#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "175c458d-36cc-5f90-b6ee-976e082c30a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8943" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: v^2, \\: u+v \\big\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$, the fundamental vector product is defined as $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$.\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u + v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2u, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u + v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 2v, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2v,-2u, 4uv⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "1b4b69f7-fc4f-57c3-bcaf-9df98e4fc5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8947" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations\n$x = u, \\quad y = v, \\quad z = 1 - u - v$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(1 - u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(1 - u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ &= ‖⟨1, 1, 1⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{3}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "4b202b4e-d8dc-524c-8cb4-ab0c00eb78cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8958" :question/problem "$AAAAAAAA$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2$. What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{3} - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0, 3u^{2}⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{3} - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3u^{2}, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ &= ‖⟨ - 3u^{2}, 1, 1⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3u^{2})^{2} + (1)^{2} + (1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9u^{4} + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{9u^{4} + 2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answ